 <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ash">A working buyer's guide to rush-order shirts — what drives the speed, what breaks a rush, and how to give yourself the best chance of hitting the deadline.</p>
 <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-hairline pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-ash"><span class="text-gold-600">Bighorn Threads Team</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span>{post.readTime}</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time></div>
 </div>
 </header>
 <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8"><img src={post.image} alt="Rush order custom shirts in production at a Las Vegas screen printing shop" width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" class="w-full border border-hairline object-cover" /></div>
 <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">

 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">Rush speed isn't a fixed number. Artwork readiness, product availability, decoration method, quantity, partner capacity, and delivery all affect timing. Send your deadline and specification. Bighorn will confirm the supported date in writing.</p>
 </aside>

 <h2 id="rush-tiers" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Drives Rush Speed</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">There's no single rush turnaround. Your timeline comes down to a few drivers. The more of these that line up, the faster the shop can move.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Small orders move fastest</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">A short rush window is realistic when:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li>Artwork is finalized, with no revisions or new file preparation</li>
 <li>The selected product and sizes are available</li>
 <li>The shop has open press or embroidery machine time</li>
 <li>Standard ink/thread colors</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Most often used for: trade show ribbons, last-minute event crews, single-day client meetings, executive last-minute swag.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Medium runs are the common rush</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Most rush jobs land here. Typical for:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li>New crew onboarding when the start date moved up</li>
 <li>Trade show booth crews ordered close to the event</li>
 <li>Replacement runs for shirts damaged in production or shipping</li>
 <li>Project-specific apparel for a job walk or ribbon cutting</li>
 </ul>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Large runs rush against a longer baseline</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Bigger quantities carry a standard bulk timeline, so "rush" on a large run means compressing that baseline rather than turning it around overnight. Compressing it still takes queue priority and coordinated production scheduling — call with your quantity and deadline and the shop will tell you what's achievable.</p>

 <h2 id="what-breaks" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Actually Breaks a Rush Order</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Five constraints make or break rush turnaround:</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">1. Artwork readiness</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">If a logo needs embroidery file preparation, that adds work before production. Screen-print color separation can add work too. The quote confirms setup, artwork requirements, and whether an approved file can support the order.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">2. Blank garment availability</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Product and size availability can change. Sourcing and inbound shipping may add lead time. Specialty protective garments also require exact manufacturer documentation and employer approval.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">3. Color matching</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Color matching and material sourcing may add time. Confirm the approved color standard, partner tolerance, and available materials before relying on a deadline.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">4. Production queue</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Production capacity changes by partner and date. Bighorn confirms available partner capacity and the supported delivery date in the written quote.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">5. Thread spec for compliance work</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Protective garments may require specific thread, backing, placement, and decoration methods. Your employer and the garment manufacturer must approve the complete specification. Confirm material availability before relying on a rush schedule.</p>

 <h2 id="local-vs-national" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Local Vegas Shop vs National Online Rush Services</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">National services and local account teams may use different production and shipping paths. Compare each provider's supported delivery date, complete specification, and written terms.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">A local account team can compare available production and handoff options for a Las Vegas deadline. Confirm the production location, pickup or delivery path, and supported date in writing before you commit.</p>

 <h2 id="how-to-rush" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">How to Maximize Your Rush Chances</h2>
 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Ask early.</strong> More lead time gives the account team more partner, product, and delivery options.</li>
 <li><strong>Send finalized artwork.</strong> Vector format (.ai, .eps, .pdf), no revisions, no "we might want to change the colors." Lock the spec before quoting.</li>
 <li><strong>Confirm product availability.</strong> Ask the provider to confirm the product and required sizes before committing to the timeline.</li>
 <li><strong>Keep an approved specification.</strong> A documented garment, artwork, placement, colors, and sizes can reduce clarification on a reorder. File availability and retention still require confirmation.</li>
 <li><strong>Be honest about the actual deadline.</strong> "Need by Friday" gives the shop room to optimize; "need yesterday" doesn't help anyone.</li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="related" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Related Reading</h2>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
